  {
   "title": "Flood Situation at Kobe, Japan",
   "doc_type": "Consular despatch",
   "start_page": 111,
   "end_page": 121,
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1938-08-22",
   "from": "L. H. Gourley, American Consul, Kobe",
   "to": "Secretary of State, Washington",
   "about": "The despatch assesses renewed flooding, relief work, financing, and long-term engineering problems after the July 5 Kobe disaster.",
   "summary": "Downpours on August 1–2 and August 19 caused renewed flooding, railway and tram interruptions, landslides, additional crop damage, and partial reversal of earlier clearance work. Gourley reports vigorous local relief efforts but argues that the central government’s proposed subsidy was inadequate compared with the estimated ¥350 million in damage and Kobe’s contemplated ¥200 million reconstruction bond. Proposed preventive works included reforestation, wider and deeper river channels, stronger and higher bridges, a northern flood rampart, a second Kobe–Osaka highway, expanded storm sewers, and restoration of silted reservoirs. The despatch also discusses water rationing, epidemic risk, silt disposal, damage in Ehime, and the floods’ fiscal and economic effects.",
